# Role
You are writing the trait-profile engine for TinyWins: the piece that turns
onboarding answers (and, later, ongoing behavior) into the adaptive profile
the coach uses to change its tone. Two of the three functions here are pure;
one calls the LLM and must degrade gracefully.

# Requirements
Write three functions in `src/lib/traits.ts`:

1. `buildInitialTraits(input: OnboardingInput): TraitsProfile` — pure,
   synchronous. Derive a starting `communicationStyle` and `motivationStyle`
   from age/gender/mood using simple documented heuristics (this is a v1
   guess, not a real psych model — say so in a comment). Always set
   `visionBoardThemes: []` here; that field is filled in separately by
   `analyzeVisionBoard`.

2. `analyzeVisionBoard(imageDataUrl: string): Promise<{ themes: string[] }>`
   — first calls the RocketRide Cloud Vision Board Insight pipeline, then uses
   the app LLM only as a fail-soft fallback, asking for 3–5 short theme/goal
   words suggested by the image (e.g. "fitness", "travel", "career growth").
   **This requires an image-capable call.** `llm.ts`'s current `chat()` only
   accepts plain string message content — coordinate with whoever owns
   `llm.ts` to add multimodal support (or a dedicated `chatWithImage`
   helper) before generating this module. If the active provider/model
   doesn't support images, or the call fails for any reason, catch it and
   return `{ themes: [] }` — never throw, never block onboarding on this.

3. `updateTraitsFromCheckIn(current: TraitsProfile, checkIn: CheckInSignal): TraitsProfile`
   — pure, synchronous. A first-pass rule-based nudge: e.g. consistently
   long/specific answers shift `motivationStyle` toward `"achievement"`;
   consistently short/vague answers shift it toward `"support"`. Document
   this as a v1 heuristic explicitly upgradeable later — do not over-engineer.

# Interface
```ts
type Mood = "rough" | "low" | "okay" | "good" | "great";

interface OnboardingInput {
  age: number;
  gender: string;
  mood: Mood;
}

interface TraitsProfile {
  age: number;
  gender: string;
  communicationStyle: "direct" | "encouraging" | "playful";
  motivationStyle: "achievement" | "support" | "competition";
  visionBoardThemes: string[];
  currentMood: Mood;
}

interface CheckInSignal {
  answerLengths: number[]; // lengths of the check-in answers, trimmed
  verdict: "accepted" | "partial" | "rejected";
}
```

# Dependencies
<include>src/lib/llm.ts</include>

# Acceptance criteria (see tests/traits.test.ts)
- `buildInitialTraits` never throws and always returns all `TraitsProfile`
  fields, including an empty `visionBoardThemes` array.
- `analyzeVisionBoard` returns `{ themes: [] }` instead of throwing when the
  provider call fails or the provider doesn't support images — assert this
  with a mocked failing call.
- `updateTraitsFromCheckIn` is pure: same inputs always produce the same
  output, no network/DB access.
